TYPE_STATE — compile-time lifecycle check

Marker: TYPE_STATE_PATH_A_IN

What is true
  Line-start directives name a machine and the transitions.
  check refuses a call that needs a state the name does not have.
  if/else: both sides must end in the same state for each tracked name.
  if without else: the missing side keeps the old state.
  return / process_exit ends that arm. It does not join.
  match arms join like if/else. A return arm drops out.
  while/for: the body must leave each tracked name in the same state.
  A transition in an if or loop condition refuses.
  return expr is checked, then that arm ends.
  First param of a From -> To fn starts as From.
  If the body text calls another TYPESTATE fn (any arm, including s.fn()),
  every live exit must leave that param as To.
  A leaf body (no nested TYPESTATE call in the body text) trusts the directive.
  A From -> To fn cannot rebind its first param name.
  Ctor -> To has no param. process_exit does not require To.
  let t = s and t = s share one lifecycle. connect(s) updates send(t).
  State names belong to the last TYPESTATE: Type. Sock Open and File Open can both exist.
  // TYPESTATE end Closed on a machine: main must leave those names in Closed.
  s.connect() is the same rule as connect(s).
  Imports keep TYPESTATE lines (small graphs).
  Files with no TYPESTATE fn line are unchanged.
  Check cache keys include ts3 so an old OK is not reused.

Forms
  // TYPESTATE: Sock Closed Open
  // TYPESTATE fn open_sock: -> Closed
  // TYPESTATE fn connect: Closed -> Open
  // TYPESTATE fn send: Open -> Open
  // TYPESTATE fn close_sock: Open -> Closed

State names are global. Two machines must not share a state name.
First arg of a transition must be a name, not a nested call.

Not this
  type_state_check / typestate_assert free calls stay refused.
  No heap alias. No inter-file lattice.

